Hail rgrn,

this is the YAAP for a character I ascended some time ago. I just
recently stumbled over it while searching for my current game's notes,
and remembered I hadn't posted this right away as the group was
already being spammed with enough trash at that time (yes Rob Cypher,
I'm pointing at you) and I haven't bothered reading all that much here
since. Now that the spam flood seems to have calmed down, I guess it's
finally time to present to you some of my own:

YA(S)AP - Ugluz the orcish rogue, geno/wish/polyitemless. With the "S"
standing for a little bit of style involved.

Ugluz the orcish rogue ascended in +6 black dragon mail, shirtless,
dual-wielding named crysknives and using poisoned shuriken as backup
ranged weapons. Plus, he brought a nice collection of souvenirs, did
not wish for anything, didn't genocide anything and didn't polymorph
any items. He *did* polymorph some monsters, but that's beside the
point of the conduct, right? He had also never changed form until very
late in the game, when he had an unfortunate chamaeleon-related
accident in Gehennom :(


I knew that if I would play a rogue some time, some minor style/
roleplaying would be involved, basically meaning some non-standard
ascension items, for reasons of memories dating back to my MMORPG-
playing days. Well, after ascending Grawl the barbarian (see my YAFAP)
and a Gruumsh the caveman (no YAAP since it was a super-boring game,
essentially a copy of the barbarian, only with crappy starting
equipment), having a tourist killed by Pestilence on the astral plane,
and having an archaeologist, a healer, an extinctionist wizard and now
a crappy priest (expect YAAP/YASD soon!) in progress, I decided to try
and play a rogue. And keep some conducts while we're at it. It worked
quite well. In retrospect, it would probably have been more stylish to
use orcish daggers instead of elven ones, but as daggers were my main
weapons, I kind of had to go with quality here. Maybe I'll try racial
conduct after ascending every role at least once, which essentially
means somewhere around the year 2027, I guess.


** Note that this YAAP is about as long and detailed as it can
possibly get. You've been warned. **



Ugluz had rather nice starting stats (STR 16, DEX 18, CON 15) when he
entered the dungeon with his little kitten Skarya at his side. Maybe
that's what made him different from the other, weaker orcish rogues of
mine who had all perished without getting anywhere far in the dungeon.
Or maybe it was the insane amount of luck he brought with him. Who
knows.

As usual by now, I decided to let my kitten do most of the fighting at
the start for cheap divine AC in Minetown. Ugluz was wounded badly
twice on the first level of the dungeon when Skarya was having a snack
instead of protecting her master, but his god Kos saved him both times
and made him even stronger in the process (HP: 11->13, then 13->16),
which helped a bit.

A small general store on DL2 was robbed clean. It didn't actually
yield anything terribly useful, but some nice-to-have-anyway stuff,
and I was playing a rogue after all, plus it was just so convenient
and easy to do with a bag. After DL4, I decided to take a detour to
the mines. Luckily, ML1 was lit. A dwarf kindly provided some nice
armor (+2 dwarvish mithril) and a mattock, which I used to dig out a
vault on DL3. I also found an oil lamp on the level, but didn't need
it as ML2 was lit as well. ML3 was Minetown. The altar was
crossaligned (lawful), but at least I managed to pull off the
protection racket at XL:1, donating 3 times for 4 points of AC (total
-11). My cat got badly wounded in a fight with the watch captain and
then practically killed herself by attacking a large mimic in the
hardware store. Ugluz was on his own now.

So I started flinging orcish daggers to exercise my skills. Some
random hobgoblin dropped a mysterious helmet, but it was cursed, so I
didn't try it on (turned out to be a HoOA later). When I accidentally
kicked a door, the watch got mad at me and I had to zap a wand of
digging downwards to escape. I went back up and killed 'em one at a
time. Got a silver saber in the process and used it as my meelee
weapon for quite some time. I advanced dagger to expert and saber to
skilled, then later ditched the saber for a longsword and advanced
that to skilled as well (a mistake, didn't use it much and should have
saved the skill slot for broadsword, i.e. Stormbringer). I also tamed
a new large cat, but lost her quickly to a soldier ant and didn't
bother with pets anymore after that.

So I went back to the dungeon and stumbled upon a bones pile on DL5:
Fnord the elven ranger, cause of death unknown (he had died in a
corridor and there was no gravestone). The corpse had a bit of cash,
another silver saber, some scrolls, rings, wands and potions, and - a
"Lolly Gooble Bliss Bomb", which made a nice souvenir (as most of the
time, you only find "slime molds" in Hearse bones piles). There was
also a general store here, which had a silver spear! First time I ever
saw one of those, I think. I carted the loot to Minetown for BUC
testing, and one of the wands turned out to be speed. Of course, I
promptly zapped myself.

After emptying a rather large leprechaun hall on DL7, I had enough
money for another point of divine AC. DL8 had the Oracle, but nothing
special otherwise. I didn't bother breaking the statues for books,
being a stupid orc and all. On DL9, I found an oilskin cloak and
another mysterious helmet which turned out to be a helm of brilliance.
I saved it for later, went to DL10 and found myself surrounded by a
horde of monsters in the middle of the Bigroom (the one with lots of
niches on the sides), so I decided to go back up and do Sokoban
first.

Sokoban provided the amulet of reflection and, besides the usual junk,
a +5 ring of protection! w00t! I went back to Minetown, donated for
another point of AC and descended further down to get the luckstone. A
random dwarf volunteered to identify amulets of live saving for me. I
found a cloak of invisibility, but kept my oilskin cloak on for level
3 cancellation.

ML7 had another bones pile: Jax the elven wizard, killed by a burning
potion - thanks to the nearby hobbit with the wand of fire, I guess.
From his body, I picked a very burnt CoMR (which later turned out to
be pre-enchanted to +5!), a magic lamp, lots of scrolls, 3 spellbooks,
some potions, rings (an uncursed ring of levitation among them, very
nice) and wands. I kept the cursed lamp as a light source.ML9 was the
bottom level, mimic version. I quickly retrieved the luckstone and
left the mines.

Back at my makeshift stash on DL2, I poisoned a stack of 71 darts for
the chance of instakilling tough monsters, but turned out too lazy to
even use one of them. I went down to the bigroom again, where I found
a tinning kit, a bag of holding and gauntlets of power right there on
the floor. Cool.

Having found a pair of lenses somewhere, I decided to read the various
spellbooks I had accumulated. One of them was a spellbook of ID!
Unfortunately, failure rate was at 99%, even with a robe on. But I
remembered some cheap spellbooks in Minetown, so I went there, bought
'em and read 'em. One turned out to be detect monster, which was just
what I had been looking for. At 10% failure, it served as a nice
practice spell for the school of divination. Soon I enhanced
divination spells to basic, which lowered my failure rate with ID
considerably, though I can't remember the exact number. I still had to
wear my robe to cast it, but I didn't mind. Having ID as a spell is
just great, no matter what you have to do to actually cast it.

I explored the dungeon some more. Found another lawful temple on DL12,
but didn't have enough money for more protection yet. Then I had a
very dangerous experience on DL13: mind flayer while fainting! Oh
crap. Luckily, I was able to burn an Elbereth, eat a food ration and
then kill it with a salvo of daggers. No brain damage suffered, phew.
DL14 had a coaligned altar, finally, and a small beehive. DL15 housed
the quest portal. I stopped exploring here and went to relocate my
stash from DL2 to DL14, sacced some stuff in between the trips and
received Stormbringer, which I used as my new meelee weapon. Enhanced
broadsword to basic, but no more, as my roleplaying-based skill plan
didn't allow for it. A donation in the temple on DL12 got me another
point of protection. Total AC was -21 now. After bringing down all my
loot to DL14, I started identifying assorted items. An Aleax showed up
and dropped a shield of reflection, but I had the amulet, so I kept an
elven shield for its lower weight. I rustproofed my GoP with a c?oDA
and went to explore the rest of the dungeon down to Medusa.

DL16 had a throne room. I sat on the throne twice, but it vanished
without giving me anything. On DL17, I found a tiny leprechaun hall
(only 4 leprechauns) and the portal to Fort Ludios. That had to wait
until later, though. DL18 was the rogue level, and for the first time,
I guess, I didn't find a cheap plastic AoY there. DL19 and 20 had
nothing special, DL21 had a rather large treasure zoo, where I also
found my 2nd bag of holding. I took a quick detour to the priest on
DL12 for the last two points of divine AC (naked AC 1 now, total -26),
then decided to have a quick look at my quest home level, where I was
promptly greeted by the rare woodchuck (just a chamaeleon, though). I
left as quickly as I had come in, as I noticed this was a maze-like
level and I absolutely wasn't in the mood for this right now.

I wanted to explore some more, but DL22 just happened to be Medusa's
island. Killed the titan and a gremlin infestation, then noticed
Stormbringer was blasting me, so I went back up to DL14 to fix my
alignment (damn peaceful chaotic multiplying gremlin suckers. hrmph.).
The sacfest provided a wraith, and it left a corpse! Hello XL:14.
After saccing one /oCM's worth of critters, my alignment was fine
again and I went to do the quest anyway.

Whoa, those chamaeleons sure are annoying. One of them changed into an
arch-lich and summoned nasties. The good thing: a black dragon was
among them, and it left scales. I hadn't found a ?oEA so far, though.
Stormbringer attacked one of the quest friendlies, so I had to
polymorph him (into a kobold) to kill him without f***ing up my
alignment again. I had big trouble with another arch-lich (a polytrap-
powered leprechaun this time) on the middle level. He cursed my stuff
and summoned nasties quicker than I could kill them. I scattered a
large bunch of them around the level by breaking a wand of teleport,
but he just kept summoning more, so I had to escape via c!oGL. After
uncursing and re-blessing my stuff on DL14, I returned, burned
Elbereth on the stairs and suddenly the mighty foe turned into a minor
annoyance. Clearing up the horde of nasties on the level yielded so
much loot that I returned to my base camp once again before facing the
Master Assassin, to stash all the potentially useful items and spend
my only ?oEA found so far to turn my black scales into dragon scale
mail.

Then I dug down to my nemesis' home, beat him up a bit and scared him
to the stairs, explored and looted the rest of his home area, then
quaffed a c!oGL, went down the stairs and easily finished him off.

Back in the dungeon with the Master Key of Thievery and the silver
bell, it was time to check out the castle. On Medusa's level, I found
a magic marker (0:61) and used it to write some more scrolls of
enchant armor. Perseus's statue had a cursed silver shield, which I
left there, as I already had an uncursed one and didn't need it
anyway. There were three maze levels (did I mention I HATE those?)
below Medusa, where I found Mjollnir, cursed -1 speed boots (which I
enchanted and wore immediately) and a mind flayer who managed to
brainsuck me before I could get him to suck up some of my daggers.
Argh! I forgot lots of level maps (and some items, but the ID spell
took care of that) and went up to DL1 to remap everything.
Incidentally, some monster I killed on the way dropped a spellbook of
magic mapping. I enhanced divination spells to skilled, but the
failure rate was still too high, so I had to keep on mapping the
forgotten levels the hard and slow way. The priest of Issek on DL12
accidentally fell to the wrath of Stormbringer... but I was going to
get rid of him sooner or later anyway, so what.

At the castle, I pushed the boulder from the maze section into the
moat to create a bridge, but it just sank, so I put on my ring of
levitation and went in through the back door. The storerooms had
nothing of value, but I got two wands of death in the throne room. The
throne summoned an audience twice, then vanished. Well, more loot at
least, better than nothing. I looted the rest of the place including
the barracks (the wand was 0:3, but I didn't need anything, so I
figured I'd go wishless), then went back to my stash. There, I
alchemized 15 potions of full healing to buff my HP a bit, just to be
on the safe side (231 HP now).

The Valley of the Dead had a large number of wraiths, I lured them all
past the castle up to DL25 and got a total of 10 corpses. Yeah, I know
you can lure them *down* instead, but there was so much stuff coming
at me from the left side (thanks to a lich casting aggravate monster
or whatever, thus waking the whole level), I figured there was no way
to get to the downstairs without slogging through hordes of assorted
undead first.

After clearing the valley and getting 7 candles from Minetown, I
prepared to rush through Gehennom, when I remembered I still hadn't
done Ludios! I didn't actually need the money, but went there anyway,
for the sake of completeness. It turned out to be a great decision, as
the place provided 3 more wands of death! I also got some grey and
silver scales, which I took with me (just in case...). The throne
vanished after sitting on it twice with no effects, as usual.

Back at my stash once again, I fireproofed and enchanted my stack of
42 elven daggers to +7 and also made myself a fixed +7 crysknife.
Stormbringer's thirst for blood and souls was getting annoying and
most of the stuff in Gehennom was level-drain resistant anyway. Plus,
ascending with a crysknife had style. Then I enchanted my black dragon
scale mail. The first scroll must have brought it from +2 to +4 or +5,
but I didn't notice and just read another scroll! I lucked out though,
and now I was the proud owner of a +6 black dragon scale mail! Phew. I
also alchemized some !oGA to max out my stats for casting magic
mapping in Gehennom. Usually, I dig out all the mazes, but I just
couldn't be bothered with it this time.

In the meantime, an ice troll showed up and kindly provided yet
another wand of death. I offered his corpse, prayed to make some holy
water and got crowned :/ .. which effectively gave me nothing. I
already had Stormbringer and all the intrinsics, but well, it had to
happen eventually, though I had only prayed 4 or 5 times so far (side
note: I never prayed again after this. You hear that, Kos? I got the
message.).

Time to practice my knife skills a bit. I collected a stack of 21
knives from Ludios and the castle and started throwing them at ghosts
I summoned in the now deserted temple on DL12. Soon, I was expert in
knife. Then I dual-wielded my crysknife with a +1 silver saber and
enhanced two-weapon combat to expert as well. After that, I created
some sacrificing fodder on Dl14. I wanted to summon Yeenoghu and
Juiblex to face them on my terms. After emptying three wands, I
finally got a hill orc to offer. Yeenoghu appeared, I used a hard gem
to engrave Elbereth on the upstairs and whacked him.

On the way back to the altar, some gremlin hit me and chuckled. "Crap,
I've just lost an intrinsic", I thought. However, there was no
additional message about *which* intrinsic had been stolen. I promptly
asked the kind folks at r.g.r.n. about this. While waiting for an
answer, it came to me that it could have been my intrinsic
disintegration resistance: no additional message because I was still
disintegration resistant from wearing the black scale mail? So my new
goal was to summon a black dragon to eat and see if I was right. I
emptied 6 wands and a 7th one dropped by a summoned stalker, got Frost
Brand and Ogresmasher in the process (thank you so much, Kos), but no
dragon. Luckily, someone at r.g.r.n. told me I hadn't, in fact, lost
anything, as the gremlin had apparently targeted some intrinsic I
didn't have at that time. Thanks again for clearing that up!


Now it was finally time to conquer Gehennom. Before literally going to
hell, I had decided to return to the quest levels and farm nymphs for
their potions of object detection, when I noticed an unidentified
spellbook in my stash. I put on my lenses, read it and - learned the
detect treasure spell. Ok, so I forgot about nymph farming and went
straight into Gehennom. There, I cast magic mapping and detect
treasure on every level, pickaxed paths between the stairs and
retrieved the valuable items, then went down to the next level.
Here's a brief summary of special events:

DL31: Juiblex' swamp. Get engulfed, zap wand of digging, whack him
once, apply unihorn. Barely counting as a special event.
DL32: Asmodeus' fortress. He wanted some 600$, I refused, engraved
Elbereth on the stairs and killed the greedy sucker.
DL34: Baalzebub's lair, same as above.
DL35: another wand of death right there on the floor next to the
stairs. I stopped counting them.
DL38: Stairs to Vlad's tower. We all know he's a wimp, so nothing
really special here either. The throne vanished immediately (what
else?). Somewhere in the tower, I finally found a scroll of charging,
which I used to recharge my marker. Wrote two more ?oEA and enchanted
my GoP.
DL39: Orcustown. I dug past it, as I felt I really didn't have enough
wands of death yet! :)
DL40: some arch-lich dropped an athame for me! Yay for easy one-turn
engraving. Also a 2nd amulet of life saving.


On DL40 I had another very unpleasant accident. I was hungry and
something big, I think a wumpus or baluchiterium, came along. I typed
"e" to eat it and "y" to confirm... and when I noticed I was eating a
chamaeleon corpse, it was already too late. I turned into a black
dragon, lost my cloak of magic resistance and my polyself conduct :
( Plus, I was stranded on DL40 without magic resistance. The only good
thing was that I could easily disintegrate everything the RNG threw at
me until I reverted back to my original form.
Luckily, after some time, a nurse finally dropped a scroll of
teleport, so I confused myself with a long forgotten spell and
teleported to my stash on DL14.


Okay, so what to do? I wanted to keep my wishless and polyitemless
conducts, and keep wearing my +6 black dragon scale mail for style
purposes. But I needed a new source of MR. Then I suddenly remembered
that priests sometimes wear cloaks of MR! I armed myself with a wand
of probing, took some grey dragon scales with me for emergency MR
(good thing I picked 'em up back at Ludios), and went to check out the
remaining two priests in the game. The minetown priest was wearing an
ordinary robe, but Moloch's priestess in the Valley did indeed have a
CoMR! I put on my grey scales and mercilessly slaughtered her in a
storm of blessed +7 elven daggers. And as if a new CoMR wasn't enough,
she also left me a new spellbook of ID, which was nice, as my original
one only had one "charge" left.

I went to my stash, used up my magic marker on 2 ?oEA and enchanted my
new cloak to +5, then fireproofed it with a cc?oDA. I also upgraded a
worm tooth to a second crysknife, but didn't use it just yet, as I
still had the pickaxe in my 2nd weapon slot for digging paths between
the stairs.


Back to Gehennom!
DL44: got double-teamed by a master lich and an arch-lich near the
stairs and had to get backup daggers out of my BoH. The arch-lich
again had an athame, this time even a +3 one. A cockatrice showed up
and left a corpse, I promptly returned to Orcustown and slapped Orcus
with it. Got his wand of death fully charged and found another silver
spear in one of the shops (as I said, this was the first game I ever
saw one of those!).
DL45: Entrace to Rodney's tower. Had a quick look, beat up some kind
of lich inside, then left.
DL47: Found a 2nd pair of speed boots, Snickersnee and a figurine of a
demilich, which I took with me as a souvenir.
DL48: Another arch-lich, another athame. What's up with you, RNG? Also
a glass orb, but I left it there. Too heavy.
DL49: Vibrating Square. Marked it, put the pickaxe into my BoH and
started dual-wielding my crysknives, then went back to my stash for
some ascension run preprations.

First, I took my 120.000$ to Minetown and donated them to the priest.
10 donations equaled 1 additional point of AC. Meh. Then I decided to
empty my 2 bags of tricks and the remaining 4 wands of create monster,
hoping that some monster brought along more scrolls of EA or even a
magic marker. I left 1 charge in every wand and then broke them, to
maximize the number of monsters. Nothing. I checked out the lower
levels of Rodney's tower (caused conflict there and whacked stuff with
a rubber chicken as there were some disenchanters among the crowd),
found some more wands of CM and a single ?oEA. Not enough. I dug out
every grave I could find, found some items, but nothing useful.

Finally, I cursed all my scrolls of CM (35 in total) and went to
Vlad's tower to have a mass slaughterfest. Which took a few thousand
turns, as the monsters I summoned dropped lots of additional CM wands
and scrolls. Got an oilskin sack on the way, then Sunsword from an
angel and 1 scroll of EA during the first session (after which I went
to my stash to store some of the loot. The second session produced a
spare CoMR and Demonbane (from an archon), and then a black naga
finally brought a magic marker (0:85) to the party. I stopped the
killing spree right there, as I was getting slightly bored (boredom
leads to carelessness, carelessness leads to DYWYPI!) and returned to
my stash.

After the obligatory scroll of charging, I wrote up some scrolls of
enchant weapon and enchanted both my crysknives to +7. The remaining 5
charges were used on a scroll of gold detection as I had only found
two of those. I recharged the marker and wrote up some more ?oEW,
which I used to enchant and rustproof a stack of 50 poisoned shuriken.
Some ghost killing in the deserted temple on DL12 followed, to enhance
my skill with the throwing stars. Finally, I used my last two ?oEA on
my helm of brilliance, rustproofed it and wore it instead of the rusty
+3 helmet I'd been wearing until now.

I put all the souvenirs I wanted to take with me in a sack,
miscellaneous planes equipment like wands of digging and teleportation
in another sack, both sacks in a bag of holding and placed that next
to the stairs on DL1, ready to pick it up before leaving the dungeon.
After some last-minute preparations, I grabbed the invocation
artifacts and it was finally time to start the run! Here's the short
summary with turn numbers:

123901: tried to levelport down to DL45, landed in the Valley instead,
duh.
124032: tried again from DL28 and succeeded, entered the Wizard's
tower
124305: traded with Rodney (1 death ray for 1 Book of the Dead, thank
you) and went back down to DL45
124481: levelported to DL49, did the invocation, went into the Sanctum
and back up
124603: levelported down to the Sanctum, had to bash my way through
the graveyard and some priests anyway, used a /oSD to find the door
124679: got the Amulet, escaped via c!oGL, then started climbing up,
using more c!oGL to skip annoying levels
125028: mysterious force, DL37->DL38
125111: The Wizard appears and dies
125138: mysterious force, DL34->DL35
125149: see above
125178: dito, but I stayed on DL35 this time
125257: mysterious force kept me on DL33
125384: arrived in the Valley with 1 c!oGL left, phew
125578: Rodney turns up and, for a change, gets shredded to pieces by
two blessed fixed named +7 crysknives
125658: used my last potion to skip Medusa's island, noticed I had
forgotten to dig paths between the stairs in the dungeon
125842: minor annoyance: an archon on DL14
126203: climbed the stairs to the plane of earth. Rodney gets to eat
death ray again, first wand is empty now.

I decided to save my 3 scrolls of gold detection for the other planes
and started digging paths between the caverns. Got a scroll of
charging here, blessed it and put in in my BoH, just in case I ran out
of death charges. Rodney appeared and got killed one last time on turn
126448. I found the portal via autosearching in the 6th cavern,
dropped my pickaxe and all but one wand of digging, put on the ring of
levitation and entered.

The Plane of Air was easy. I confused myself and detected the portal.
The elementals really aren't scary with half physical damage. Entered
the Plane of Fire on turn 126741. The portal there was quite far away,
so I blinded myself and levitated over to it. Had to kill an archon on
the way, entered the portal to Water on turn 126806. There, I detected
the portal on the other end of the plane. Oh joy. I got hungry and
stuffed myself on a shark corpse, a rare meal indeed. When the portal
floated right next to me on turn 126926, I hit it without taking off
my ring of conflict. Don't need no freakin' guardian angels.

I arrived on the Astral Plane. Famine was right, Pestilence in the
middle and Death was on the left. Being a rogue, i.e. being greedy for
points and additional artifact loot, I went left first, as all four
player monsters were there. The first one to fall was a knight with
Fire Brand, but not before he had a chance to put it to good use and
kill Death for me.

Second was a priestess with a silver dagger (not an artifact, I know,
but a nice souvenir nevertheless), then finally a rogue and a tourist
without anything special. The altar was lawful, so I killed the high
priest of Issek and went back to the middle. Death quickly succumbed
to my mad dagger throwing skillz several times, though he did manage
to touch me once.

On the way, some giants appeared, and I used their boulders to trap
Death in the left part of the level.

A ki-rin in the middle summoned quite a horde of monsters, but nothing
troublesome. Pestilence was somewhere in the far back of the temple
area. I killed everyone and everything in my way, gave Pestilence a
death ray and... was standing in the high temple of Kos.
Unfortunately, the death ray had hit his high priest as well... so bye-
bye divine protection. I decided to rectify this capital sin by taking
a detour to the third altar first and eliminating the high priest of
Mog there. Somewhere on the way, a disenchanter was summoned right
next to me and my current wand of death was empty! There was no time
to get a new one out of my bag, so I poly'd him into a violet fungus
instead. Famine died, thanks to my ring of conflict, in the middle of
a huge skirmish between priests and angels. The high priest got a
death ray, as usual. On the way back, another giant appeared and
dropped a boulder - now all three riders were blocked off. Hell yeah!
The astral plane was mine to rule!

So it was finally over. Ugluz took out the little figurine he had
found in Gehennom, blessed and activated it so he would have a
companion in heaven. Bones the demilich watched in awe as his orcish
master lit the candelabrum once more, kneeled in front of Kos' altar,
offered the amulet and was bathed in radiance.


Conclusion #1: An extremely lucky game indeed. More luck than skill,
considering the countless situations that could have easily ended the
game right there, or at least made it considerably more difficult.
Conclusion #2: Throwing daggers rule. They just rule. I had never
really believed all the fuss people were making about throwing daggers
as compared to meelee weapons, but guess what, they were right.
Conclusion #3: I had always way overrated rings of slow digestion.
Never found one in this game and did totally fine without it.


My third ascended game was a bit different and definitely more intense
than the two previous ones. I had to cope with "holy crap" situtions
and the lack and/or loss of critical items - yep, this game taught me
strategic thinking and patience, it taught me that no situation is
truly hopeless, however bad it might look at first.
In the end, I found myself vastly over-prepared again, I probably
could have made the ascension run with much less HP, a smaller stack
of daggers/wands, without those poisoned missiles I never used anyway,
etc.

All in all, though, the game provided both a very entertaining and
educating experience. Sorry for the extremely long story, but hey, I
was keeping detailed notes throughout the game, so why not turn those
into an equally detailed YAAP?


Best regards,
C.


